  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several visible mechanisms by which small molecules can create misleading or nonspecific biological effects. One major theme is colloidal aggregation: above a critical aggregation concentration, individual compound particles cluster into large aggregates that can trap proteins and cause partial or local denaturation. Another mechanism is metal chelation, illustrated with a polyfunctional molecule binding a divalent metal ion through nearby oxygen atoms. The image also highlights membrane disruption, where a lipid bilayer that starts intact becomes distorted and penetrated by a compound, implying nonspecific damage to membranes.\n\nA second broad theme is compound instability and off-target chemistry. One panel shows a parent aromatic compound converting into a reactive product that can attach to cysteine, representing breakdown products or impurities that react covalently with biomolecules. The last mechanism concerns optical interference: a heterocyclic aromatic structure is shown interacting with wavy blue light, indicating compounds that either quench a signal or emit their own signal, thereby distorting assay readouts.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that apparent activity in experiments can arise from assay interference and nonspecific physicochemical behavior rather than true target-specific biology.",
